  "query_preview": "..\n\nIn the mid-levels this morning, a high pressure is centered \nover southern California and southern Arizona. As the high sits \nin a similar spot to yesterday, mean winds and subsequently \ntoday's thunderstorm chances will be similar and remaining near\nand east of a line from Hannagan Meadow to Tucson to Nogales. \nSatellite derived precipitable water values this morning are \nlargely unchanged in the 1.1-1.25 inch range, which will combine\nwith near 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE to produce an isolated flash \nflooding, severe wind gust, and blowing dust threat. Most 00Z \nhigh resolution model data keeps the strongest thunderstorm \nchances (as well as severe potential) east of Tucson today, \nfocused across Graham, southern Greenlee, and Cochise into Santa\nCruz counties.\n\nThe mid-level high will undergo a transition northward this\nweekend, turning mean flow over southeastern Arizona to an east\nto west direction. This shift will bring richer moisture into\nthe region with precipitable water values increasing to 1.25-1.5\ninch range. Additionally winds aloft will strengthen producing \naround 20-25 kts of bulk shear, enhancing a severe and flash \nflooding threat with the potential for storms to become more\norganize or cluster. Coverage of chances are more widespread \nwith this wind shift as 25-50 percent chances cover southeastern\nArizona with 40-60 percent chances near the International \nBorder. A similar setup is forecast to be in place on Sunday as \nwell, though some variability will be possible in coverage and \nlocation from Saturday.\n\nBy the middle of next week, the ridge center will shift north\nand east with the strongest positive height anomalies centered \nover the Northern Plains. This will shift mean winds over\nsoutheastern Arizona a bit between the east to northeast, but \nthunderstorm chances will remain in some fashion daily next \nweek. With this increased activity high temperatures are \nforecast to lower to near normal from this weekend into next \nweek, potentially dipping below normal depending on thunderstorm\nactivity.",
